::: {.doc-subject}

Add a table stub, to emphasize row and group information.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_modify_rows.py#L48-L122){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.tab_stub(
    rowname_col=None,
    groupname_col=None,
)
```
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[rowname_col]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column to use for row names. By default, no row names added. A list of columns
    creates a hierarchical stub: the last column holds the row names, and the columns before
    it are outer levels (outermost first), with repeated values merged across adjacent rows.

<code>[groupname_col]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column to use for group names. By default no group names added.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The GT object is returned. This is the same object that the method is called on so that we
    can facilitate method chaining.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

By default, all data is together in the body of the table.

::: {#f7299730 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, exibble

GT(exibble)
```

